We recently completed two sessions with SymphonyAI as part of our ongoing vendor landscape assessment.

The first session, held last month, provided a high-level overview of their agentic AI pipeline with a strong emphasis on the front end. The second, held last week, was a deeper technical walkthrough of their back-end architecture.

Overall, the engagement was positive — the SymphonyAI team were collaborative and open, and the discussions were genuinely useful for benchmarking purposes. That said, the key takeaway is one of validation rather than differentiation: their back-end architecture is broadly equivalent to what we have developed internally, and their agentic AI approach mirrors what has been published by firms such as PayPal. The one notable difference is the use of an additional agent to generate HTML output, designed to ease integration with their case management system — an interesting design choice, though case manager-dependent and unlikely to accelerate our own integration path.

In summary, SymphonyAI's case management capability is mature, but their back-end does not contain anything we believe would meaningfully improve narrative quality or investigation speed beyond our current capability. Their solution operates at the same level of investigation automation as ours.

Happy to discuss further if useful.
